<rdf:RDF xml:base="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/agentic-orchestration/"
	xmlns="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/agentic-orchestration/"
	xmlns:AnnotationVocabulary="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FND/Utilities/AnnotationVocabulary/"
	xmlns:agento="http://www.w3id.org/agentic-ai/onto#"
	xmlns:cmns-av="https://www.omg.org/spec/Commons/AnnotationVocabulary/"
	xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
	xmlns:dct="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
	xmlns:fibo-fnd-gao-obj="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FND/GoalsAndObjectives/Objectives/"
	xmlns:oe-agtorch="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/agentic-orchestration/"
	xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
	xmlns:pplan="http://purl.org/net/p-plan#"
	xmlns:process="http://www.daml.org/services/owl-s/1.2/Process.owl#"
	xmlns:prov="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#"
	xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
	xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
	xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
	xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/"
	xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#">
	
	<owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/agentic-orchestration/">
		<rdfs:label>Agentic Tools and Skills Orchestration Ontology</rdfs:label>
		<dct:abstract xml:lang="en">Large language model (LLM) agents now extend themselves in two ways: through tools, exposed by external servers like Model Context Protocol (MCP) and command-line interfaces, and through skills, modular packages of instructions and resources the agent loads to perform specialized tasks. As both multiply, agents must decide which tools and which skills to invoke, in what order, under what dependencies, and against which user preferences, and they rarely explain those choices. We present the Agentic Capabilities Ontology, an OWL 2 DL ontology that acts as a semantic layer between the user and the agent harness. It treats tools and skills as parallel providers of capabilities, describing each one&apos;s inputs, outputs, preconditions, dependencies and constraints, and recording tool invocations and skill invocations alike as provenance. The ontology reuses AgentO, P-Plan, PROV-O and OWL-S, and models requests and their information requirements, hard and soft user constraints, candidate orchestration plans composed of workflow steps, and plan evaluations scored on decision criteria such as tool call count, token cost and evidence coverage. Defined classes and property chains let a reasoner infer which tools and skills satisfy which requirements, how steps depend on one another through the information artifacts they consume and produce, and which plan is selected. We evaluate the ontology on a personalized supplement-recommendation use case with four skills and four external servers, answering nine competency questions with SPARQL over the inferred knowledge graph, covering plan selection, required tools and skills, parallel execution, constraint violations, prerequisite readiness, and explanations of why each was chosen.</dct:abstract>
		<dct:creator>Barbara Kotlan</dct:creator>
		<dct:creator>Elioenai Lincon Guedes Alves</dct:creator>
		<dct:creator>Gia Oriana Santos</dct:creator>
		<dct:creator>Jialin Fang</dct:creator>
		<dct:creator>Jiaying Wang</dct:creator>
		<dct:license rdf:datatype="&xsd;anyURI">http://opensource.org/licenses/MIT</dct:license>
		<dct:modified>November 20, 2026</dct:modified>
		<dct:publisher xml:lang="en">Team Agentic Capabilities</dct:publisher>
		<rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://tetherless-world.github.io/ontology-engineering/branch/agentic-orchestration/oe2026/agentic-orchestration/gettinginvolved"/>
		<owl:imports rdf:resource="http://purl.org/net/p-plan#"/>
		<owl:imports rdf:resource="http://www.daml.org/services/owl-s/1.2/Process.owl"/>
		<owl:imports rdf:resource="http://www.w3id.org/agentic-ai/onto"/>
		<owl:imports rdf:resource="https://www.omg.org/spec/Commons/AnnotationVocabulary/"/>
		<owl:imports rdf:resource="https://www.w3.org/ns/prov-o.owl"/>
		<owl:priorVersion rdf:resource="&oe-agtorch;v4.0"/>
		<owl:priorVersion rdf:resource="&oe-agtorch;v4.1"/>
		<owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="&oe-agtorch;v4.2"/>
		<owl:versionInfo>v.4.2</owl:versionInfo>
		<skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">Version 4.2, November 20, 2026; changes from version 4.1. Header: owl:priorVersion is now the version IRI of version 4.1 instead of a link to the archived Assignment 10 file, owl:versionIRI and owl:versionInfo carry version 4.2, and the default namespace declaration (xmlns) was removed from the RDF/XML header; every term is written with a prefix, so no triple changed. Removed the import of FIBO Goals and Objectives (https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FND/GoalsAndObjectives/Objectives/), which no axiom, individual or competency question query used and which brought in 22 further FIBO and OMG Commons ontologies. The OMG Commons class Measure, the superclass of plan metric, came only through that import and is now declared in this ontology. OOPS! pitfall P04 (unconnected elements): the domains and ranges that P-Plan, PROV-O and OWL-S give pplan:correspondsToStep, prov:used, prov:wasGeneratedBy and process:hasParameter, and the subclass axioms pplan:Plan rdfs:subClassOf prov:Plan and prov:Plan rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity, are restated unchanged, so the reused classes pplan:Activity, pplan:Step, pplan:Plan, prov:Entity and process:Process are connected in this file; prov:Activity, prov:Plan and process:Parameter are declared with annotations from their sources. These axioms were already entailed through the imports, so no inference changes. The OMG Commons class MaterialMeasure, which plan metric was declared disjoint with, was removed together with that disjointness axiom: without the Commons QuantitiesAndUnits module no individual can be a material measure, so the axiom had no effect, and OOPS! reported the class as unconnected (pitfall P04). HermiT and Pellet infer the same subclass, type and object property facts for the terms and individuals of this ontology as before. Domains and ranges (OOPS! pitfall P11): the helper properties is request, is tool, is skill, is hard constraint, is soft constraint and is preference setting now have their own class as domain and range; has capability has the domain skill or tool, and uses tool the domain LLM agent or skill. IRIs imported from other ontologies were left alone for interoperability. Added missing annotations to IRIs flagged by OOPS! (most of which are imported from other ontologies). Version 4.2 also adds a second owl:priorVersion (version 4.0, Assignment 10) and links the header to the maintenance and evolution policy on the team website (rdfs:seeAlso and skos:note).</skos:changeNote>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Maintenance and evolution policy: https://tetherless-world.github.io/ontology-engineering/branch/agentic-orchestration/oe2026/agentic-orchestration/gettinginvolved</skos:note>
		<cmns-av:copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Team AgenticCapabilities</cmns-av:copyright>
	</owl:Ontology>
	
	<owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="&dct;bibliographicCitation">
	</owl:AnnotationProperty>
	
	<owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="&dct;creator">
	</owl:AnnotationProperty>
	
	<owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="&dct;license">
	</owl:AnnotationProperty>
	
	<owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="&dct;source">
	</owl:AnnotationProperty>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="&pplan;Activity">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">activity</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Garijo and Y. Gil, “The P-Plan ontology,” version 1.3, Mar. 12, 2014. [Online]. Available: http://purl.org/net/p-plan</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">[P-Plan] class Activity</dct:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">execution process that carries out a planned step of a plan</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Assignment 9: row added; the main ontology uses pplan:Activity as the parent of Invocation.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">An invocation of the PubMed search_articles tool that carries out the evidence-verification step of a plan.</skos:example>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Parent class of invocation in this ontology; P-Plan makes it a subclass of prov:Activity.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">P-Plan 1.3: “A p-plan:Activity represents the execution process planned in a p-plan:Step.”</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Reused from P-Plan; drawn in Figures 1b and 7a.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="&pplan;Plan">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="&prov;Plan"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">plan</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Garijo and Y. Gil, “The P-Plan ontology,” version 1.3, Mar. 12, 2014. [Online]. Available: http://purl.org/net/p-plan</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">[P-Plan] class Plan</dct:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">entity representing a set of steps that one or more agents intend to carry out to achieve a goal, in which each step uses and produces variables</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Assignment 9: definition added (the row had none).</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Ken plan 1, a candidate orchestration plan with ten workflow steps, is a P-Plan plan.</skos:example>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">P-Plan 1.3: “A p-plan:Plan is a specific type of prov:Plan. It is composed of smaller steps that use and produce Variables.”</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Reused from P-Plan; drawn in Figure 5a.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="&pplan;Step">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">step</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Garijo and Y. Gil, “The P-Plan ontology,” version 1.3, Mar. 12, 2014. [Online]. Available: http://purl.org/net/p-plan</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">[P-Plan] class Step</dct:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">planned execution activity that forms part of a plan</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Assignment 9: definition added (the row had none).</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Step 1 of Ken plan 1 (get user profile) is a P-Plan step through orchestration workflow step and AgentO workflow step.</skos:example>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">P-Plan 1.3: “A p-plan:Step represents the planned execution activity.”</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Reused from P-Plan; superclass of agento:WorkflowStep, drawn in Figure 5a.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="&pplan;correspondsToStep">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">corresponds to step</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Garijo and Y. Gil, “The P-Plan ontology,” version 1.3, Mar. 12, 2014, class Activity and property correspondsToStep. [Online]. Available: https://www.opmw.org/model/p-plan/</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">P-Plan ontology (D. Garijo and Y. Gil, 2012; http://purl.org/net/p-plan#), term correspondsToStep</dct:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="&pplan;Activity"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="&pplan;Step"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">links an execution activity to its planned workflow step</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Traceability: AC-001_ConceptualModel_TermListTraceability.xlsx, Properties!A6, Figure(s) 1, 7. AI-assisted definition adapted for the use case (note moved from Explanatory Note in Assignment 10).</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">An evidence-retrieval invocation corresponds to the plan&apos;s evidence-retrieval step.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">P-Plan connects runtime activities to their planned steps.</skos:note>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain → range as used here: invocation → workflow step. The declared rdfs:domain and rdfs:range (P-Plan activity → step) are restated unchanged from P-Plan 1.3, so the reused classes they name are connected in this file (OOPS! pitfall P04); this ontology adds no narrower domain or range.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">Adapted from P-Plan ontology (D. Garijo and Y. Gil, 2012; http://purl.org/net/p-plan#), term correspondsToStep.</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The model specializes the endpoints to Invocation and WorkflowStep.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
		<cmns-av:usageNote xml:lang="en">Link each invocation in an execution trace to the planned workflow step it carries out.</cmns-av:usageNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="http://w3id.org/beam/core#Resource">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">resource</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">A. Ekelhart, K. Kurniawan, F. J. Ekaputra, and E. Kiesling, &quot;AgentO: An ontology for modeling agentic AI systems,&quot; in The Semantic Web: 23rd European Semantic Web Conference, ESWC 2026, Dubrovnik, Croatia, May 10-14, 2026, Proceedings, Part II (Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol. 16550), M. Acosta, M. van Erp, S. Rudolph, O. Hartig, B. Spahiu, A. Rula, D. Garijo, and F. Osborne, Eds. Cham, Switzerland: Springer, 2026, pp. 298-320, doi: 10.1007/978-3-032-25159-6_16. Page numbers refer to the 22-page author preprint linked from the ESWC 2026 accepted-papers page (https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WrMSiXR-COZHrAKCEN6nE1M5QUB98gim/view).</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">K. Kurniawan, “AgentO: An ontology for modeling agentic AI systems,” Zenodo, Jan. 22, 2026, doi: 10.5281/zenodo.18342625. [Online]. Available: https://zenodo.org/records/18342625 [Accessed: Sep. 22, 2026].</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">R. Iannella and S. Villata, “ODRL Information Model 2.2,” W3C Recommendation, Feb. 15, 2018. [Online]. Available: https://www.w3.org/TR/odrl-model/</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">“The BEAM ontology (Boxology&apos;s Extended Annotation Model),” version 0.1.0, Feb. 5, 2026, class Resource. [Online]. Available: http://w3id.org/beam/core [Accessed: Nov. 13, 2026].</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">[B] AgentO ontology v0.2 (Turtle) (beam:Resource, reused from BEAM), Zenodo doi:10.5281/zenodo.18342625; [A] Ekelhart et al., AgentO, ESWC 2026 (author preprint), Table 4, p. 19</dct:source>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Asset</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">asset that an agent or tool uses to perform a task, or that a task produces as its output</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Definition drafting/editing method (moved out of source fields): Claude Fable 5.1 (Anthropic, in Claude Code) - the term, its source passages (local copies of the cited documents), the definitions already in this sheet and the use case were provided; the model drafted a genus-plus-differentia definition and the notes, each quotation was matched mechanically against the source text, and the team member reviewed the result. Assignment 12: declared with the annotations of its term-list row, which the ontology file did not carry (OOPS! pitfall P08).</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">The user&apos;s health profile; the candidate-supplement list produced by the Supplement Advisor MCP; the PubMed literature index.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">&quot;Any asset that can be utilized by an agent or tool to perform tasks&quot; ([A] Ekelhart et al., AgentO, ESWC 2026 (author preprint), Table 4, p. 19); &quot;Relates a task to resources needed for its execution&quot; ([B] AgentO ontology v0.2 (Turtle), requiresResource).</skos:note>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">In AgentO a resource is anything a task requires or produces (data, files, models, instances). In this ontology the information content consumed and produced by capabilities is modeled as Information Artifact (row 14); Resource is the broader class.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">[A] Ekelhart et al., AgentO, ESWC 2026 (author preprint), Table 4, p. 19; [B] AgentO ontology v0.2 (Turtle): beam:Resource rdfs:subClassOf pplan:Variable (no rdfs:comment); requiresResource and producedResource comments</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">AgentO reuses beam:Resource and links it to tasks with requiresResource (&quot;Relates a task to resources needed for its execution&quot;) and producedResource (&quot;Relates a task to resources produced as output&quot;); tools reach resources through resourceUsage.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
		<cmns-av:usageNote xml:lang="en">Use Resource for anything an orchestration step needs or yields; use Information Artifact for the information content itself; use Resource dependency (row 24) for the relation between steps that follows from it.</cmns-av:usageNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="&process;Parameter">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">OWL-S parameter</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Martin et al., “OWL-S: Semantic markup for web services,” W3C Member Submission, Nov. 22, 2004, sec. 5.1. [Online]. Available: https://www.w3.org/submissions/OWL-S/</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">OWL-S Process ontology (http://www.daml.org/services/owl-s/1.2/Process.owl), class Parameter</dct:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">variable of a process through which the process receives an input value or returns an output value</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Assignment 12: added because the range of process:hasParameter is restated from the OWL-S Process ontology (OOPS! pitfall P04). The term has no row in the term list; the label and source were taken from the OWL-S Process ontology, whose comment does not define the class, so Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic, in Claude Code) drafted the definition from its subclasses Input and Output, and the notes.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">The query parameter of the PubMed search articles tool.</skos:example>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Labeled OWL-S parameter to keep it apart from this ontology&apos;s own class parameter (Parameter), which is not asserted equivalent to it.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:directSource xml:lang="en">OWL-S 1.2 Process ontology, class Parameter, rdfs:comment: “This is the simplest way to relate parameters to SWRL (and DRS) variables.”</cmns-av:directSource>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Reused from OWL-S as the range of process:hasParameter; OWL-S makes Input and Output subclasses of it.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="&process;Process">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">process</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Martin et al., “OWL-S: Semantic markup for web services,” W3C Member Submission, Nov. 22, 2004, sec. 5. [Online]. Available: https://www.w3.org/submissions/OWL-S/</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">OWL-S Process ontology (http://www.daml.org/services/owl-s/1.2/Process.owl), class Process; OWL-S W3C Member Submission (2004), section 5</dct:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">specification of the ways a client may interact with a service</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Assignment 12: the term has no row in the term list; the label, definition and source were taken from the source ontology to resolve the OOPS! missing-annotation pitfall (P08), and Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic, in Claude Code) drafted the notes.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">The PubMed search articles tool, which is an OWL-S process through the class tool.</skos:example>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">OWL-S, section 5: “It is important to understand that a process is not a program to be executed.”</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">OWL-S W3C Member Submission (2004), section 5: “It is a specification of the ways a client may interact with a service.”</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:directSource xml:lang="en">OWL-S 1.2 Process ontology, class Process, rdfs:comment: “The most general class of processes”</cmns-av:directSource>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Reused from OWL-S; superclass of tool, drawn in Figure 4, so the OWL-S properties process:hasParameter and process:hasPrecondition apply to tools unchanged.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="&process;hasParameter">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has parameter</rdfs:label>
		<dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Martin et al., “OWL-S: Semantic markup for web services,” W3C Member Submission, Nov. 22, 2004, sec. 5.1. [Online]. Available: https://www.w3.org/submissions/OWL-S/</dct:bibliographicCitation>
		<dct:source xml:lang="en">OWL-S Process ontology (http://www.daml.org/services/owl-s/1.2/Process.owl), term hasParameter</dct:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="&process;Process"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="&process;Parameter"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relationship linking a process interface to a formal parameter through which values are supplied or returned</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Traceability: AC-001_ConceptualModel_TermListTraceability.xlsx, Properties!A15, Figure(s) 4. AI-assisted definition adapted for the use case (note moved from Explanatory Note in Assignment 10).</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A search tool has a query parameter.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">OWL-S defines a Process-to-Parameter relation.</skos:note>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain → range as used here: tool → parameter. The declared rdfs:domain and rdfs:range (OWL-S process → parameter) are restated unchanged from the OWL-S Process ontology, so the reused classes they name are connected in this file (OOPS! pitfall P04); this ontology adds no narrower domain or range.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:adaptedFrom xml:lang="en">Adapted from OWL-S Process ontology (http://www.daml.org/services/owl-s/1.2/Process.owl), term hasParameter.</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Tools take parameters (tool → parameter); reusing the OWL-S property carries its process domain.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
		<cmns-av:usageNote xml:lang="en">Use for tool parameters; tools are OWL-S processes, so the OWL-S domain applies.</cmns-av:usageNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
